WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.170
- [Narrator] Both of its wings
have transparent windows.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.827
- Crystal duck.

00:00:06.827 --> 00:00:07.660
(audience laughing)

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:08.493
- Open the Southern border.

00:00:08.493 --> 00:00:09.326
(voices overlapping)

00:00:09.326 --> 00:00:11.003
- Care about now more coronavirus cases

00:00:11.003 --> 00:00:12.453
than any single country in the world.

00:00:12.453 --> 00:00:13.286
(voices rapidly overlapping)

00:00:13.286 --> 00:00:14.119
- NFTs are.

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:17.211
(rapid gunfire)

00:00:17.211 --> 00:00:21.294
(voices chaotically overlapping)

00:00:26.744 --> 00:00:28.800
- The leak is not our main concern, is it?

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.290
- [Joey] I feel like I wanna
jump out of my own skin.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.123
- I just

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.290
- [Joey] That's the type of thought

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.880
that runs through my brain

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.623
when I feel overloaded, overstimulated,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.160
when I'm jacked in.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.330
Keeping up with every
little thing that's going on

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.450
in the world, it's like a pressure,

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:50.974
a tension.

00:00:50.974 --> 00:00:51.807
- No, no, no, no.

00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:52.640
- [Joey] Caving in my skull.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.870
- That's good, just
keep rubbing your head.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.543
- I nurse on the input, like a newborn.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.180
It's the first thing I
reach for when I wake,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.700
the last thing I look at before I sleep.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.810
And yeah, I know it's not good.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:09.040
I'd rather be doing something
else, but I can't help

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.923
but feel that if I let it
go, I'll miss something.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.813
I'll feel disconnected, alone.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.950
But the pressure builds.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.170
This extension of myself.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:26.140
It revs my mind up and it doesn't stop.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.940
It's like I'm looking for something.

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:35.579
(birds chirping)

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.300
One thing that I've started to notice

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.840
is that whenever I cut
myself off from the feed,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.290
when I choose to be alone,
when I seek stillness,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.123
I find it.

00:01:52.594 --> 00:01:54.040
And this feeling I was running from

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.853
was what I was searching for.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.870
Life is often exactly as
simple as we need it to be.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.260
There's nothing complicated
about a flowing stream,

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.870
a crackling fire, a cloudy day.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.493
Unless of course we make it that way.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.780
We can always analyze
life, cerebralize it,

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.383
theorize about it if we need to.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.180
But how often do we need to?

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.680
Are things not also
meant to be experienced

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.663
for what they are, as they are?

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.920
Because, obviously thinking about things

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.810
and analyzing reality
helps us understand it.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.630
But when we live in our
heads, it distorts reality.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:42.640
We start looking with our
minds instead of our eyes.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.230
The mountains don't care
what we think of them.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.834
(wind howling)

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.834
(raindrops tapping)

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.150
There's a quote by GK Chesterton

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:59.067
that's always stuck
with me, goes like this.

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:01.987
"It is an act of faith to
assert that our thoughts

00:03:01.987 --> 00:03:04.487
"have any relation to reality at all.

00:03:04.487 --> 00:03:06.087
"If you're merely a skeptic,

00:03:06.087 --> 00:03:09.047
"you must sooner or later
ask yourself the question.

00:03:09.047 --> 00:03:10.947
"Why should anything go right,

00:03:10.947 --> 00:03:13.527
"even observation and deduction?

00:03:13.527 --> 00:03:17.417
"Why should not good logic be
as misleading as bad logic.

00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:20.910
"They are both movements in
the brain of a bewildered ape."

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.660
I always thought this
quote was pretty funny

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.960
and it does a great job of
reminding us that what happens

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.360
inside our brains may
not be as sophisticated

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.160
as we probably think.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.500
We tend to celebrate the human mind

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.860
as the pinnacle of intelligence.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.210
Yet, when we spend too much time in there,

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.880
we become anxious and isolated.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.050
Why is that?

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.910
Why is it that the most
common piece of advice

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:48.640
that you might hear for
someone who's socially anxious,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.860
needlessly vengeful, or
compulsively conspiratorial

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.750
is get outta your head.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.800
It's like we all instinctively know

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.840
that there's a disconnect
between reality itself

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.860
and our interpretation of it.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.720
And when we spend too much
time untethered from the world

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.890
as it is, and instead
filter our experiences

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:12.100
through references and
distortions, isn't it obvious

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.000
that the resulting
effect is disorientation?

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.510
Is it not obvious that
when we plug ourselves

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.910
into sources that profit off
stirring up fear and dread

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:24.750
within us, that we would feel
more worried and dreadful.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.180
I always find it hilarious.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.200
It'll be a beautiful day
outside, the most peaceful,

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.200
divine reality before us

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.070
but we'll be on YouTube
or Twitter or Reddit.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.700
and there'll be some
world event that usually

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.120
has nothing to do with us

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.910
and we'll go, that's terrible.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:41.560
Man, the world is (beep) up.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.840
And then we look out the
window at the glorious sun

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.060
piercing through the clouds
over a rolling landscape

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.270
and instead of wonder,
or awe, or gratitude,

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.190
we think, would I
survive a nuclear winter?

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.950
I'll leave you guys with a quote.

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:57.350
Marcus Aurelius said
that if you're distressed

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:00.110
by anything external, the pain is not due

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.100
to the thing itself but
to your estimate of it

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.013
and this you have the power
to revoke at any moment.

